testing libstdoc
===============

The tests are registered with CTest and run with:

    cmake -S . -B build
    cmake --build build
    ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure

Run a subset by name:

    ctest --test-dir build -R heap --output-on-failure


What is registered today
-------------------------

    index_output          golden output of index.c
    format_output         golden output of format.c
    differential          format.c's engine against a reference C library,
                          case by case, at test time
    error_handling        failure paths (self-checking)
    stream_output         staging, discard and stream order, merged
                          golden output of stream.c
    large_write_is_reported
                          one 200000 byte write, checked for status,
                          length and bytes on a file and on a pipe
    heap_checks           allocator, safe variants, leak counts
    heap_abort_overflow   safe size overflow terminates with 134
    heap_abort_oom        safe out of memory terminates with 134
    heap_abort_double_free
                          safe double free terminates with 134
    heap_abort_invalid_pointer
                          safe bad pointer terminates with 134
    index_cxx_output      the same golden output, from C++
    install_and_consume   the library installs, and a separate project
                          builds and runs against the installation

The last test builds the whole library a second time, installs it into
a scratch directory under the build tree and configures a small consumer
project against that directory; it takes a great deal longer than the
others and is the only one that touches the filesystem outside the build
tree's own subdirectories.


The kinds of test
-----------------

Golden output
    The program prints, CompareOutput.cmake runs it and requires two
    things: an exit status of 0 and standard output identical to the
    golden file in test/.  A test that fails without printing anything
    is still reported, because the status is checked as well.

    Used by index_output, format_output and index_cxx_output.

Merged golden output
    The same, except that both streams are merged into one file before
    they are compared, so the golden file records where a diagnostic
    written to stderr lands relative to output written to stdout.
    CompareMergedOutput.cmake does the redirection and the comparison.

    Used by stream_output.

Reference comparison
    One source is compiled twice: once linked against libstdoc and once
    against the host C library, with -DSTDOC_REFERENCE choosing the
    calls.  CompareTwo.cmake runs both programs and requires them to
    print the same bytes.  Nothing is stored, so the reference is a live
    one and cannot drift into being a snapshot of libstdoc.

    Used by differential (test/differential.c).  Its cases are built at
    run time, so every combination of flags, width and precision is
    covered - over two thousand cases today - without the file growing
    with them.

Self-checking
    The program performs its own checks, prints "FAIL: ..." for each
    one that does not hold, and returns the number of failures as its
    exit status.  Zero is a pass, so no golden file is needed.

    Used by error_handling (test/errors.c) and heap_checks
    (test/heap.c).

Fatal paths
    A scenario that the library is documented to terminate with: the
    program names the scenario on the command line, does something
    invalid, and must end inside libstdoc with status 134.
    CheckFatal.cmake asserts exactly that status, so a scenario that
    exits cleanly, exits with a different status or crashes fails the
    test instead of passing unnoticed.

    Used by the four heap_abort_* tests (test/heap_abort.c).

Write destinations
    The program performs one large write and CheckLargeWrite.cmake runs
    it twice, once with standard output on a regular file and once with
    standard output on a pipe.  Each run has to report success, and the
    bytes that arrive have to be exactly the bytes the program produced,
    compared with the pattern the program uses rather than with a golden
    file of two hundred thousand bytes.

    Used by large_write_is_reported (test/largewrite.c).

Installation
    InstallTest.cmake configures, builds and installs libstdoc into a
    scratch prefix, checks that the headers, the archive and the package
    metadata are where the install rules promised to put them, then
    configures test/consumer against that prefix with find_package,
    builds it, runs it and checks what it printed.  When the host has
    pkg-config, it is asked to describe the package as well.

    Used by install_and_consume.


Golden files
------------

test/expected-format.txt was produced from test/format.c by a reference
C library rather than by libstdoc itself, so the comparison is a real
check and not a snapshot of whatever the implementation happens to do.
Regenerate it from the repository root with a hosted compiler:

    cc -DSTDOC_REFERENCE -w test/format.c -o reference
    ./reference > test/expected-format.txt

test/expected-index.txt matches what index.c prints.

test/expected-stream.txt is different in kind: it is the merged output
of test/stream.c run against libstdoc, and it states a property that
only libstdoc defines - that stdout is flushed when a newline is staged
while stderr is unbuffered - so a reference C library is the wrong thing
to generate it from (glibc buffers stdout to a file and would interleave
the two streams differently).  Regenerate it with the same redirection
the test uses, checking the merged result before committing it:

    /bin/sh -c './build/test/test_stream > /tmp/merged 2>&1'
    cat /tmp/merged
    cp /tmp/merged test/expected-stream.txt
Regenerating any golden file changes what the library is held to:
review the diff before committing it.


Rules a new test follows
------------------------

  * Freestanding tests compile assembly/start_aarch64.S into
    themselves, so linking never depends on the linker recovering a
    symbol from an archive, and link with -nostdlib -static.
  * Every C test uses the warning policy the library is built with,
    with -fno-builtin and -fno-stack-protector.  Two programs are the
    documented exception: test/format.c and test/errors.c are built
    with -Wno-format, because a good part of what they pin down - the
    '0' flag on a string, a precision on %p, a '+' on an unsigned
    conversion, an empty format string, a width literal too large for an
    int - is behaviour this library defines on purpose and "-Wformat"
    refuses to compile.  The reason is stated again where those cases
    are, in the files themselves.
  * A test that needs internals includes the internal header of that
    layer (io/stdoc_io.h or mem/stdoc_mem.h) by adding the directory
    to that target only.  errors.c does this on purpose: a stream
    bound to a bad descriptor cannot be obtained through the public
    interface, and its sticky-error behaviour is worth testing.
    stream.c uses the same header for stdoc_stream_discard, and
    largewrite.c for stdoc_sys_write.
  * A test that checks a value carries its own verdict (exit status),
    so it needs no golden file.
  * A program whose output is compared with a golden file writes
    nothing else to standard output; a note for a reader goes to
    standard error, which the merge in CompareMergedOutput.cmake places
    where it belongs and the golden files record.


The leak policy of the heap tests
---------------------------------

The allocator tests use small allocations only, so anything a bug loses
stays small, and the accounting in mem/stdoc_mem.h turns any leak into a
failing check: after every scenario the live byte and block counts must
be back at zero.  The exhaustion scenario deliberately fills the arena
to prove that a full heap behaves - stdoc_malloc reports it, printf
falls back to its stack buffer, and releasing everything restores the
whole arena.

The tests do not depend on the arena size: they ask the allocator what
it has and shape the scenario accordingly, so the same sources run
against any arena the build accepts.  Building with -DSTDOC_HEAP_SIZE=64
and running the same tests is a supported and useful variation, and the
configuration guard rejects anything below 64.


What is not tested
------------------

  * The retry for a short write in io/syscall_linux.c is not covered by
    an automated test.  A short write cannot be produced on demand: a
    blocking write completes the whole transfer before it returns, and
    the cases that do return early - a terminal whose output has been
    stopped and interrupted by a signal, a non-blocking descriptor -
    need signal handling or O_NONBLOCK, neither of which the library has
    a wrapper for.  large_write_is_reported covers what can be checked
    (the status, the length and the bytes of a write far larger than the
    stream buffer, on a file and on a pipe); the retry itself is
    defensive and is exercised only by inspection.
  * The count-versus-error boundary of stdoc_kernel_result is covered
    only through stdoc_sys_write.  stdoc_sys_read has the same
    classification and no caller yet; when the input layer arrives, its
    own test has to cover a read of 4096 bytes or more.
  * Sanitizers and valgrind are not run: a "-nostdlib" freestanding
    program has no runtime for them.  Leak detection uses the
    library's own accounting instead.
  * Only the host that builds the project (aarch64 Linux) is
    exercised.  No other operating system or architecture is claimed
    to work without a toolchain and a successful build.
  * The comparison against a reference C library covers the
    conversions both implement.  Where they are documented to differ,
    the difference is pinned by a golden file instead; see
    docs/formatting.
